WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.044
- We don't teach history in schools.

00:00:03.044 --> 00:00:06.589
So the fact that we
condense Black history

00:00:06.589 --> 00:00:11.094
all in one month when Black history
is American history, it's like we

00:00:11.094 --> 00:00:15.640
do ourselves a disservice, not only
for, not only for the legacy of Black

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:19.352
people and what we’ve actually contributed to
this country, but for the perspective

00:00:19.352 --> 00:00:22.981
of white people to know, you know,
that this is their history as well.

00:00:22.981 --> 00:00:27.777
That the things that were done to
Black people is what is the white legacy.

00:00:27.777 --> 00:00:28.695
- [Taylor Rooks:] I'm Taylor Rooks.

00:00:28.695 --> 00:00:33.825
And these are conversations about
defining one's relationship to Blackness

00:00:33.825 --> 00:00:36.369
and how that defines their America.

00:00:36.369 --> 00:00:36.995
OK.

00:00:38.246 --> 00:00:39.122
OK, Malcolm.

00:00:39.122 --> 00:00:45.170
So I am starting all of these with the
exact same question, which is:

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:49.090
When did you realize that being Black
was different from being white?

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:51.634
- I didn't realize it probably until

00:00:51.634 --> 00:00:53.470
the third grade

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:58.391
because I started school at a private
school that was all Black

00:00:58.391 --> 00:01:01.519
in the city of Newark, New Jersey.
It was called Chad School.

00:01:01.519 --> 00:01:06.024
And I laugh now because I'm like,
it was almost like they were, it was

00:01:06.024 --> 00:01:09.778
like an academy for Black Panthers or
something. Like, every day we sung

00:01:09.778 --> 00:01:13.323
the Black National Anthem. Every
day was Black History Month.

00:01:13.323 --> 00:01:15.909
We called everybody brother, sister.

00:01:15.909 --> 00:01:18.244
Um, that's how we addressed our teachers.

00:01:18.244 --> 00:01:20.747
And it was like, they just
taught you so much about

00:01:20.747 --> 00:01:25.794
your, you know, your heritage, like,
having pride in being Black and,

00:01:25.794 --> 00:01:27.420
like, how to work as a community.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:34.052
And so I was there from pre-K until second
grade, and then in third grade, I moved to

00:01:34.052 --> 00:01:38.848
Piscataway, New Jersey, where I spent the
rest of my time and went to public school.

00:01:38.848 --> 00:01:44.771
And it was, you know, a stark difference, all the way
from just not seeing any Black teachers

00:01:44.771 --> 00:01:50.110
in front of you to the curriculum,
you know, being totally different to

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:53.571
even, like I said, the National Anthem
and what you saw, what you sung

00:01:53.571 --> 00:01:56.741
kind of every day—those routines and
rituals were totally different.

00:01:56.741 --> 00:02:00.995
And I think that representation,
um, that you usually, that I was

00:02:00.995 --> 00:02:03.123
used to seeing was no longer there.

00:02:03.123 --> 00:02:07.335
And I think that's kind of when I began
to see the difference between,

00:02:07.335 --> 00:02:13.216
you know, something that I was, like, so
proud of and grew up kind of honoring,

00:02:13.216 --> 00:02:18.763
going into a different situation and
seeing it not necessarily be held to that same regard.

00:02:18.763 --> 00:02:22.725
- And I would say most Black people probably
did not have that experience, you know,

00:02:22.725 --> 00:02:26.855
when they're younger being surrounded
by Black people, but also Blackness.

00:02:26.855 --> 00:02:31.943
But I do want to talk about
why there is a need for exclusively

00:02:31.943 --> 00:02:34.529
Black spaces, because I think that

00:02:34.654 --> 00:02:37.740
some white people might hear this and
say, “Well, that's dividing us, having

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:41.661
you at a school that is only with Black
people.” But, and it's the same thing I say

00:02:41.661 --> 00:02:46.040
when I would hear white people say
that the TV show Blackish was racist.

00:02:46.583 --> 00:02:49.210
It's that Blackish actually isn't racist,

00:02:49.294 --> 00:02:52.797
and you guys have plenty of
programs that are whitish.

00:02:52.922 --> 00:02:57.468
They're just called things like
Friends or Seinfeld or Sex and the City.

00:02:57.468 --> 00:03:03.266
- Right. Right.
- And since the world has been so
catered to white people and what white

00:03:03.266 --> 00:03:07.854
people like to consume, you don't need
the things to explicitly be called

00:03:07.854 --> 00:03:10.648
Black because everything is white.

00:03:10.773 --> 00:03:13.568
So you don't have to name it as white.

00:03:13.568 --> 00:03:19.991
- I mean, even think about like, uh, when we
talk about, when we name different groups of people

00:03:19.991 --> 00:03:22.493
in America, we say African American.

00:03:22.493 --> 00:03:27.040
We say Asian American, Latin American,
all of these different types of American.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:30.835
But when we talk about white, the
white people, it’s just American because

00:03:30.835 --> 00:03:35.965
we've always kind of had that idea
that white is synonymous with American.

00:03:35.965 --> 00:03:36.633
- Yeah.

00:03:36.633 --> 00:03:38.551
Like, white needs no hyphen.
- Right.

00:03:38.551 --> 00:03:41.679
- They need no hyphen in their identity.
- Right.

00:03:41.679 --> 00:03:45.141
And I think, you know,
especially for

00:03:45.141 --> 00:03:46.226
Black people,

00:03:46.226 --> 00:03:49.687
we oftentimes don't have
a lot of those safe

00:03:49.687 --> 00:03:53.900
spaces. You know, when you look
at, even, even from like a commerce

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:56.986
or business standpoint, you go to
almost every major city in the country,

00:03:56.986 --> 00:04:00.531
you'll have a Chinatown, you'll
have a Little Italy, you'll have

00:04:00.531 --> 00:04:05.328
these pockets where different, you
know ethnicities, you know, have

00:04:05.328 --> 00:04:06.913
these spaces that they can retreat to.

00:04:06.913 --> 00:04:10.583
They do business with each other, and it's
not exclusive, you know? It's not like

00:04:10.583 --> 00:04:12.460
people can't come into those communities.

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:16.089
But now those are their spaces.
And I feel like Black people don't

00:04:16.089 --> 00:04:18.841
really have those kind of spaces.

00:04:18.841 --> 00:04:20.635
And so I think it's important

00:04:20.635 --> 00:04:21.469
for us to create them.

00:04:21.469 --> 00:04:24.514
I know it was important for
me to even, like, start my

00:04:24.514 --> 00:04:26.724
education in a space like that.

00:04:26.724 --> 00:04:31.896
You know, and I have friends that
I've, you know, known since kindergarten,

00:04:31.896 --> 00:04:36.526
and all of us kind of have that
same now framework and perspective on

00:04:36.526 --> 00:04:39.487
our Blackness, on our history and culture.

00:04:39.487 --> 00:04:44.284
And we see the difference between us and
our peers who had different experiences.

00:04:44.284 --> 00:04:44.951
- Yeah.

00:04:44.951 --> 00:04:49.163
And going off that, I don't remember
the exact article and the ins and

00:04:49.163 --> 00:04:53.876
outs of it, but it was discussing
how a lot of other, like, races and

00:04:53.876 --> 00:04:57.880
ethnicities have things like Chinatown,
Little Italy and things like that.

00:04:57.880 --> 00:04:58.881
But

00:04:58.881 --> 00:05:02.218
the Black neighborhoods and the
Black communities don't have those

00:05:02.218 --> 00:05:06.931
because the dollar circulates
one day and leaves the next day.

00:05:06.931 --> 00:05:10.435
Whereas in those other smaller
communities, like Chinatown

00:05:10.435 --> 00:05:13.646
and Little Italy, the dollar
stays there for, like, a year.

00:05:13.646 --> 00:05:16.107
- Well, we used to have those
things, and that's, that's the

00:05:16.107 --> 00:05:18.234
one thing that I had to learn.

00:05:18.234 --> 00:05:19.360
Cuz I asked that same question.

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:24.490
Why don't we have, like, like, our neighborhood is the hood.
Like, we don’t own anything in it.

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:27.785
Like, we, if the dollar leaves
the neighborhood, you know, why

00:05:27.785 --> 00:05:28.953
can't we build these things?

00:05:28.953 --> 00:05:32.957
And, and that's when my family
kind of taught me about Black Wall

00:05:32.957 --> 00:05:36.461
Street in Tulsa, Oklahoma, in 1921
and other communities like it.

00:05:36.461 --> 00:05:40.381
And a lot of those communities during
the Jim Crow South, where they

00:05:40.381 --> 00:05:44.427
didn't really have much—nothing near
the freedoms that we have today—

00:05:44.427 --> 00:05:45.762
they were still building wealth.

00:05:45.762 --> 00:05:49.265
They were building their own
businesses, huge communities where

00:05:49.307 --> 00:05:54.354
literally they had all classes of
people from wealthy Black folks all

00:05:54.354 --> 00:05:55.855
the way down to the working class.

00:05:55.855 --> 00:05:58.900
And they kept that, that
money into their communities.

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:01.652
And unfortunately, almost all of
those neighborhoods met the same

00:06:01.652 --> 00:06:06.157
fate. And it was either burned down or
violently dispossessed from Black

00:06:06.157 --> 00:06:08.409
people and really never recovered.

00:06:08.409 --> 00:06:12.872
And so, you know, that's, that's
one of those things when we look at,

00:06:12.872 --> 00:06:17.627
um, by today's context, we like, “Damn,
why don't Black people have these spaces?”

00:06:17.627 --> 00:06:22.882
Like, we, you almost, you’ll think that
we are doing something to, you know,

00:06:22.882 --> 00:06:26.886
that, or we aren't doing something
like everybody else when really, if you

00:06:26.886 --> 00:06:30.640
know our history, we've done this
probably better than anybody else

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:32.475
and have just had it destroyed.

00:06:32.475 --> 00:06:36.771
And then, you know, then you had all of
the legislation and other things that

00:06:36.771 --> 00:06:38.981
were put in place to keep it that way.

00:06:38.981 --> 00:06:42.944
And now here we are in 2020,
trying to figure out how to

00:06:42.944 --> 00:06:45.530
recreate those types of spaces.

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:48.658
- Yeah. And I mean, I think, and this 
is a thing me and you talk often.

00:06:48.658 --> 00:06:53.830
This is a thing that we have discussed is
the way that we tell stories is important,

00:06:54.372 --> 00:06:58.167
and the way that you start stories.
Because a lot of people had never

00:06:58.167 --> 00:07:01.337
heard of Black Wall Street until,
like, Russell Westbrook said he

00:07:01.337 --> 00:07:02.547
wanted to do a documentary on it.

00:07:02.547 --> 00:07:04.132
And some people were like, “What is that?”

00:07:04.132 --> 00:07:08.511
And it's because if you tell the
story of Black people starting

00:07:08.511 --> 00:07:11.180
with simply, like, oppression, right?

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:13.766
Or if you tell a story of Black
people starting with now, you're

00:07:13.766 --> 00:07:18.229
gonna miss the context of the
things that have happened to us

00:07:18.229 --> 00:07:22.859
that disabled us from doing many of the
things that we should be doing every day.
- Yeah.

00:07:22.859 --> 00:07:23.860
- And that takes away,

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:25.945
I think it skews the entire story.

00:07:25.945 --> 00:07:30.825
And I think in a lot of ways has skewed
the way people view Black people.

00:07:30.825 --> 00:07:31.367
- Yeah.

00:07:31.367 --> 00:07:32.994
I think that's why we've arrived now,

00:07:32.994 --> 00:07:37.290
like, and a lot of white people
struggle with the idea of racism

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:42.503
because we've, we've painted the
picture of racism to be water hoses and

00:07:42.503 --> 00:07:44.630
dogs and calling somebody the N-word,

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:46.632
and that's not the
only form of racism.

00:07:46.632 --> 00:07:48.342
You know,

00:07:48.342 --> 00:07:50.845
and there are so many
other systems that were put in

00:07:50.845 --> 00:07:55.057
place that were, that were tied and
synonymous to racism back in the day.

00:07:55.057 --> 00:08:01.105
But once we, you know, lost our stomach for
lynchings and hoses and dogs,

00:08:01.105 --> 00:08:02.982
those systems still stayed in place.

00:08:02.982 --> 00:08:04.108
And I think

00:08:04.108 --> 00:08:07.236
we don't teach history in schools.

00:08:07.236 --> 00:08:12.992
So the fact that we condense Black
History all in one month when Black

00:08:12.992 --> 00:08:16.454
history is American history, it's
like we do ourselves a disservice,

00:08:16.454 --> 00:08:20.416
not only for, not only for the
legacy of Black people and what we’ve

00:08:20.416 --> 00:08:22.418
actually contributed to this country,

00:08:22.418 --> 00:08:25.963
but for the perspective of white
people to know, you know, that

00:08:25.963 --> 00:08:28.424
this is their history as well,

00:08:28.424 --> 00:08:32.803
that the things that were done to Black people
is, is the white legacy, like, as

00:08:32.803 --> 00:08:34.805
much as we don't like to talk about it.

00:08:34.805 --> 00:08:38.643
And even, even, you know, places
like Tulsa, Oklahoma, the reason a lot

00:08:38.643 --> 00:08:42.104
of people don't know about it is because
even the state of Oklahoma didn't even

00:08:42.104 --> 00:08:46.526
recognize the incident until 2011.

00:08:46.526 --> 00:08:51.989
And so it's a lot of these things are
not, from a historical point, not things

00:08:51.989 --> 00:08:53.783
that we want to push forward as a country.

00:08:53.783 --> 00:08:57.328
And so they’re oftentimes swept under
the rug, and we try to have that

00:08:57.328 --> 00:08:59.622
reconciliation without really

00:08:59.622 --> 00:09:01.958
arriving on a common truth.

00:09:01.958 --> 00:09:06.337
And so we’re constantly, like, you know,
confused. White people are like, “I'm not

00:09:06.337 --> 00:09:09.090
racist and I don't understand racism.”

00:09:09.090 --> 00:09:13.135
And it's because they haven't
been taught about how racist systems

00:09:13.135 --> 00:09:17.098
work or how things can be skewed
or the history, and Black people,

00:09:17.098 --> 00:09:21.185
we know it because we feel it, but
oftentimes we don't even know, you

00:09:21.185 --> 00:09:25.273
know, what we were able to accomplish,
the things that we've been able to do,

00:09:25.273 --> 00:09:28.150
what does it look like to
have those kinds of group

00:09:28.150 --> 00:09:31.320
economics and those examples,
cuz they've been destroyed.
- Yeah.

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:32.863
- And we've kind of just moved on.

00:09:32.863 --> 00:09:39.370
So history, we need to really focus
on history in our schools because that's,

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:42.081
I mean, that's the hardest thing about
not knowing where to go is we really

00:09:42.081 --> 00:09:44.250
don't even understand where we came from.

00:09:44.250 --> 00:09:46.794
- You said something that I think is very important,

00:09:46.794 --> 00:09:50.631
um, and that is the core of a
lot of these clips and things

00:09:50.631 --> 00:09:52.341
that we see on social media, right?

00:09:52.341 --> 00:09:56.262
Which is what exactly is racism.

00:09:56.262 --> 00:09:59.724
Because, for example, when you saw
like, you know, Amy Cooper call the

00:09:59.724 --> 00:10:05.479
cops and, like, threaten essentially
this Black man's existence, a lot of

00:10:05.479 --> 00:10:07.898
people were like, “She's not racist.”

00:10:07.898 --> 00:10:10.318
Even she came out and
was like, “I'm not racist.”

00:10:10.359 --> 00:10:15.281
Which begs the question of, like, then
what do you think racism is?
- Well, yeah.

00:10:15.281 --> 00:10:19.493
- And I do think a lot of white
people do think racism is explicit.

00:10:19.493 --> 00:10:19.952
- Yup.

00:10:19.952 --> 00:10:23.122
- When really a lot of racism is
microaggression that’s maybe a

00:10:23.122 --> 00:10:27.168
little bit more covert. Like, racism
doesn't just exist in somebody

00:10:27.168 --> 00:10:30.129
looking at you and saying, “I don't
like you because you're Black.”

00:10:30.129 --> 00:10:35.468
That is a very small level of
what it means to be racist.

00:10:35.468 --> 00:10:39.388
So I'm curious, can you tell me
about a time that maybe you have

00:10:39.388 --> 00:10:41.223
experienced that more covert racism—

00:10:41.223 --> 00:10:42.892
I know there's tons, right?—

00:10:42.892 --> 00:10:45.686
but some times that you could pinpoint?

00:10:47.813 --> 00:10:49.899
- Uh...I mean, there's plenty.

00:10:49.899 --> 00:10:50.691
There are plenty of times.

00:10:50.691 --> 00:10:57.365
I don't like to throw around the
word “racism” loosely because I know,

00:10:57.365 --> 00:10:58.407
to your point,

00:10:58.407 --> 00:11:01.494
a lot of, the person I'm talking
to, this white person, probably

00:11:01.535 --> 00:11:04.580
doesn’t even understand what
I'm talking about when I say, “OK,

00:11:04.580 --> 00:11:06.624
that was racist” or “You're racist.”

00:11:06.624 --> 00:11:11.420
So, you know, for me, I think every
Black person has had, you know, something.

00:11:11.420 --> 00:11:13.172
Like, I've been pulled over

00:11:13.172 --> 00:11:17.968
because officers in my hometown
thought I was my brother, and they

00:11:17.968 --> 00:11:23.099
harass my brother all the time, you know, but it's
not until they know that I'm like, “My

00:11:23.099 --> 00:11:27.937
head coach in high school was a Sergeant.”
So as long as I name-drop him,

00:11:27.937 --> 00:11:31.440
I'm lucky enough to have that
kind of association, that I know—

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:35.528
they know I have somebody to, to
protect, you know, me. But I think,

00:11:35.528 --> 00:11:39.699
you know, when it comes to racism,
people have to understand that

00:11:39.699 --> 00:11:41.534
it's, it has nothing to do with—

00:11:41.534 --> 00:11:46.747
it can deal with hate, but you don't have
to hate people in order to be racist.

00:11:46.747 --> 00:11:50.543
Race has everything to do with your
prejudices and the ability to exercise

00:11:50.543 --> 00:11:54.130
some type of power, which is why you
hear a lot of Black people say Black

00:11:54.130 --> 00:11:58.843
people can't be racist.
- Right. Because it's about power.
- Because it has everything to do with

00:11:58.843 --> 00:12:03.180
power. That white woman in the park
who's able to, to call—the reason

00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:06.600
she's calling the police and saying “A
Black man has threatened me” is because

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:10.855
she knows that the system is set up
in order to react to that situation.

00:12:10.855 --> 00:12:14.483
If a white woman calls
and says that “A Black man is

00:12:14.483 --> 00:12:15.443
threatening me,”

00:12:15.443 --> 00:12:19.280
she knows the type of response that is
going to be given. History has shown that

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:24.618
from all the way from Emmett Till to,
to Tulsa being torn down because of

00:12:24.618 --> 00:12:28.664
allegedly a white woman being assaulted
in an elevator, the same thing with

00:12:28.664 --> 00:12:33.461
Rosewood, same thing with the Red Summer
of 1919—every single time, if you

00:12:33.461 --> 00:12:40.176
can—I think it’s well-known that
white women are protected by any

00:12:40.176 --> 00:12:42.303
means in this, in this country.

00:12:42.303 --> 00:12:46.140
And so while you don't have to be racist
or hate Black people, you can still

00:12:46.140 --> 00:12:48.809
utilize a racist system to your benefit

00:12:48.809 --> 00:12:52.146
if you ever get scared or
ever just get in a bad mood.

00:12:52.146 --> 00:12:55.357
And we've seen, you know, white
women really abusing that

00:12:55.357 --> 00:12:57.401
system because they know
the system is rigged.

00:12:57.401 --> 00:13:00.946
They might not, you know, participate in it,
and they might not contribute to it,

00:13:00.946 --> 00:13:03.282
but when they want to tap into it,
they know it’s there,

00:13:03.282 --> 00:13:04.200
and they've been using it.

00:13:04.200 --> 00:13:07.411
And I think that is where
the racism comes into.

00:13:07.411 --> 00:13:09.038
- There's this author whose
name is Kiese Laymon.

00:13:09.038 --> 00:13:10.498
He's fantastic.

00:13:10.498 --> 00:13:14.794
And he wrote an autobiography, or
really a memoir, and it's called Heavy.

00:13:14.794 --> 00:13:18.672
And in it, he says that the term
“white privilege” that we use

00:13:18.672 --> 00:13:23.636
is probably it should be called
“white power” because it's about this

00:13:23.636 --> 00:13:28.682
power that white people have that
enables them to move through life.
- Yup.

00:13:28.682 --> 00:13:33.979
- But calling it privilege almost,
like, minimizes what it actually is.

00:13:33.979 --> 00:13:37.900
It's something that you have that I
don't, which in turn gives you power

00:13:37.942 --> 00:13:41.737
over me and over a lot of things.
- Right. Yeah. Yeah.

00:13:41.737 --> 00:13:45.199
- And I think that there probably is some,

00:13:45.199 --> 00:13:47.451
some truth to that.

00:13:47.451 --> 00:13:52.081
I think the reason that it’s never going
to be the popular term is because “white

00:13:52.081 --> 00:13:56.293
power” also has a different definition,
which is maybe a bit elevated than

00:13:56.293 --> 00:13:58.754
exchanging it for the word “privilege.”
- Yeah.

00:13:58.754 --> 00:14:01.674
- But I do think if people realized

00:14:01.674 --> 00:14:06.303
that racism, like you said, isn't
just hate—it's about power and the

00:14:06.303 --> 00:14:10.307
way that you harness that over other
Individuals that maybe don't look

00:14:10.307 --> 00:14:15.354
like you—there'd be a much better
understanding of how we can navigate

00:14:15.354 --> 00:14:18.065
kind of away from that, that ideal.

00:14:18.065 --> 00:14:21.193
- I like that idea cuz I feel like when you
talk about privilege, like,

00:14:21.193 --> 00:14:22.528
that's a passive term, right?
- Mhmm.

00:14:22.528 --> 00:14:26.031
- Like, I can choose whether I want to
exercise the privilege or not, but

00:14:26.073 --> 00:14:29.493
when we talk about it, like, “Hey, no,
you have, you have this power,”

00:14:29.493 --> 00:14:31.370
like, that is an active word.

00:14:31.370 --> 00:14:35.291
Like, you can—and this is why
we talk about being an ally—

00:14:35.291 --> 00:14:39.044
we don't want you to, to deny
that you have white privilege.

00:14:39.044 --> 00:14:41.297
We don't want you to ignore your
privilege. We want you to use it.

00:14:41.297 --> 00:14:42.548
Like, you have the power.

00:14:42.548 --> 00:14:45.509
So we want you to either
relinquish that power to,

00:14:45.509 --> 00:14:51.307
to elevate somebody who's Black or, you
know, or in that regard, or use that power

00:14:51.307 --> 00:14:56.145
to break down these systems because you
can have more power, more leverage, more

00:14:56.145 --> 00:14:58.856
reach than a lot of your Black peers.

00:14:58.856 --> 00:15:01.025
And I think, you know,

00:15:01.025 --> 00:15:05.779
it is not a, it is not a crime or
an indictment against white people

00:15:05.779 --> 00:15:10.618
to acknowledge that you have a power
or privilege. You didn't create it.

00:15:10.618 --> 00:15:13.704
Like, it was given to you.
In this point in time,

00:15:13.704 --> 00:15:18.000
it's, it's what do you do with it.
You know, are you using that to

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:21.420
advance us as a people or
to keep us in a status quo?

00:15:21.420 --> 00:15:26.216
And I think that's kind of that—it's
a responsibility. And it's hard

00:15:26.216 --> 00:15:30.387
for people to take that on, especially,
like, as fast as this movement has kind

00:15:30.387 --> 00:15:34.683
of come and people have had to kind
of process this, but it's gonna

00:15:34.683 --> 00:15:36.435
come down to, if we want to be better

00:15:36.435 --> 00:15:39.980
as a country, it’s going to come down
to white people who have power and

00:15:39.980 --> 00:15:44.151
who have this privilege using it to
change us because we're the minority.

00:15:44.234 --> 00:15:48.113
We don't have enough votes, voices to
do it completely on our own.

00:15:48.113 --> 00:15:53.494
And it's going to come down to those
allies using their power and privilege to assist.

00:15:53.494 --> 00:15:57.456
- And one of the reasons I asked
that question off top is because,

00:15:57.456 --> 00:16:00.751
you know, that moment where you
realize being Black is different

00:16:00.751 --> 00:16:03.879
is very commonly referred
to as an awakening.

00:16:03.879 --> 00:16:07.883
And I think a lot of Black
people are actually having

00:16:07.883 --> 00:16:11.512
that awakening in this moment,

00:16:11.512 --> 00:16:14.098
and—which seems crazy.
- That is crazy.

00:16:14.723 --> 00:16:19.687
- But I can tell from some of these, like,
statements and, like, things people are

00:16:19.687 --> 00:16:22.272
saying that this is their awakening moment.

00:16:23.649 --> 00:16:24.858
Am I alone in feeling that way?

00:16:24.858 --> 00:16:26.318
Or have you felt that way at all?

00:16:26.402 --> 00:16:30.280
- I wouldn't say it's an awakening. It would
be hard for me to really, to really—

00:16:30.864 --> 00:16:32.366
and it could be true—but it would be

00:16:32.408 --> 00:16:35.119
hard for me to fathom,
just in my own experience and

00:16:35.119 --> 00:16:40.332
background, that Black people, you know,
at this moment, this is the first time

00:16:40.332 --> 00:16:43.794
they’ve recognized that their Blackness
means something in this country.

00:16:43.794 --> 00:16:48.590
Everything about our culture
is copied, is replicated.

00:16:48.590 --> 00:16:51.885
And hell don't have a guard,
but we are demonized for it.

00:16:51.885 --> 00:16:57.182
Like, we have been completely detached
from our, you know, heritage and Africa and

00:16:57.182 --> 00:17:01.186
all of our traditions. We literally
integrated ourselves into a Western

00:17:01.186 --> 00:17:02.980
world, and we've made it our own.

00:17:02.980 --> 00:17:06.775
We created, you know, great things out
of, you know, this assimilating, but

00:17:06.775 --> 00:17:12.156
for whatever reason, when you still have
that, that racism and prejudice still

00:17:12.156 --> 00:17:16.660
around, like, you're constantly butting
your head against like, well, like, “What

00:17:16.660 --> 00:17:20.372
is it?” Like, “What else do we have to do
in order to be seen as, as true people?”

00:17:20.372 --> 00:17:25.169
And I think that, that we're at that
boiling point where people are tired,

00:17:25.169 --> 00:17:30.549
tired of assimilating, and you’re starting to
see more people, you know, look back into

00:17:30.549 --> 00:17:35.220
their heritage, look back into history
and really try to find themselves because

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:42.019
for us, trying to fit in completely into
this Western kind of mentality really

00:17:42.019 --> 00:17:43.937
hasn't played out to our benefit.

00:17:43.937 --> 00:17:49.485
- I saw, I mean, obviously we know
that there has been awakening for

00:17:49.485 --> 00:17:52.571
a number of different NFL players.

00:17:52.571 --> 00:17:55.532
I think Drew Brees kind
of took the forefront

00:17:55.532 --> 00:17:58.660
of that when he was, um, getting some heat—

00:17:58.660 --> 00:18:03.207
rightfully so—for the statement
he didn't make when he was asked

00:18:03.207 --> 00:18:08.462
about the protests, and you also
became a part of that story because

00:18:08.462 --> 00:18:11.673
you posted a very powerful video

00:18:11.673 --> 00:18:14.510
talking about the
situation on your Instagram.

00:18:19.890 --> 00:18:22.392
- Drew Brees

00:18:22.392 --> 00:18:25.104
if you don't understand how hurtful,

00:18:26.105 --> 00:18:29.149
how insensitive your comments are

00:18:29.149 --> 00:18:30.609
you are part of the problem.

00:18:30.818 --> 00:18:36.156
- And, I mean, in the video, it was
emotional, and you were crying.

00:18:36.156 --> 00:18:41.912
And I know tears can be the manifestation
of a lot of different things.

00:18:41.912 --> 00:18:46.416
Like, what did the tears
represent for you in that video?

00:18:46.416 --> 00:18:48.919
Like, what emotions were those tears?

00:18:48.919 --> 00:18:53.215
- Well, honestly, I think, you know, one
thing that, that our—and it's the only reason

00:18:53.257 --> 00:18:58.512
that I posted the video is cuz I don't like
being emotional in front of people. Like, that’s

00:18:58.512 --> 00:19:00.931
not what I’m comfortable doing, but I think

00:19:00.931 --> 00:19:06.728
people have to understand how
much Black people carry just

00:19:06.728 --> 00:19:08.021
kind of underneath their skin.

00:19:08.021 --> 00:19:12.609
Like, just on a day-to-day basis, the things
that we put up with that we don't

00:19:12.609 --> 00:19:17.781
say, the things that we see constantly
and we internalize, but, you know, try

00:19:17.781 --> 00:19:21.827
to have to go into a corporate, you
know, workplace and still get along,

00:19:21.827 --> 00:19:26.206
or have to go into these spaces that
aren't for us—we aren't represented,

00:19:26.206 --> 00:19:31.879
you know—and make people feel
comfortable with who we are all the time.

00:19:31.879 --> 00:19:36.508
And then for, you know, all of the things
to kind of come to fruition as they have,

00:19:36.508 --> 00:19:38.719
you know, in the last month

00:19:38.719 --> 00:19:43.056
to—that was kind of like, it
was hurtful because this is, these

00:19:43.056 --> 00:19:46.894
are people who you think, you know,
see you for who you are and kind of

00:19:46.894 --> 00:19:48.937
see your struggles and recognize it.

00:19:48.937 --> 00:19:53.901
And it's, it was like, you know,
frustrating because I'm like, if, if

00:19:53.901 --> 00:19:57.696
the people closest to me don't know

00:19:57.696 --> 00:20:01.158
what it feels like for me to
be in this skin, then, all right,

00:20:01.158 --> 00:20:02.075
we've got a long way to go.

00:20:02.075 --> 00:20:03.452
And I think it's important.

00:20:03.452 --> 00:20:07.289
It was important for not only
Drew, but everybody who's having

00:20:07.289 --> 00:20:12.628
these, these tough conversations to
understand that these things hurt.

00:20:12.628 --> 00:20:14.963
And they've been along—
around for a long time.

00:20:14.963 --> 00:20:18.383
A lot of the stuff we internalize.
It’s not just that it happened to

00:20:18.383 --> 00:20:20.177
George Floyd in Minnesota.

00:20:20.177 --> 00:20:23.805
Like, when we see that constantly,
I see my younger brothers.

00:20:23.805 --> 00:20:26.642
I see my cousins, my uncles, my dad.

00:20:26.642 --> 00:20:28.227
Like, that could easily be—

00:20:28.227 --> 00:20:32.231
so when we see ourselves, like,
dying, we see that, that trauma,

00:20:32.231 --> 00:20:33.690
and it does something to you.

00:20:33.690 --> 00:20:37.319
And I don't think that, you know,

00:20:37.319 --> 00:20:41.490
a lot of guys are aware of kind of
that burden that people carry around, and

00:20:41.490 --> 00:20:46.161
we do it, and we still keep a smile on our
face, and we still create beautiful things.

00:20:46.161 --> 00:20:49.331
We still have culture and
we smile, but it's tough.

00:20:49.331 --> 00:20:52.167
And I think the number one thing
you've heard people say in the

00:20:52.167 --> 00:20:54.378
last month is like, “We’re tired.”

00:20:54.378 --> 00:20:59.299
And I think that's really what that
was, just like, “I’m exhausted by holding this

00:20:59.299 --> 00:21:04.179
weight that I didn't even put on myself.” You know?
- Yeah.
- So I think that’s what it is.
- Cuz I do think a lot of people

00:21:04.179 --> 00:21:06.265
saw the emotion and was like, “He's so sad.”

00:21:06.265 --> 00:21:09.268
And I was like, “No, I
think he's probably angry

00:21:09.268 --> 00:21:14.815
and hurt.” It's not like a sadness as much
as it is, like everything that, that has

00:21:14.815 --> 00:21:19.361
come with it, you know? It's just been
an accumulation kind of of everything.

00:21:19.361 --> 00:21:23.115
Do you think Drew
now has a better grasp?

00:21:23.115 --> 00:21:23.907
- I do.

00:21:23.907 --> 00:21:25.075
I mean, yeah, I do.

00:21:25.075 --> 00:21:28.036
And I think, you know,
I think it came in, in phases.

00:21:28.036 --> 00:21:34.418
I honestly don't think he
really, you know, necessarily

00:21:34.418 --> 00:21:36.378
knew what he wasn't saying.

00:21:36.378 --> 00:21:38.547
And I, and I tried to tell
him, it's like, “Your

00:21:38.547 --> 00:21:40.382
thoughts on the flag are yours.

00:21:40.382 --> 00:21:42.968
You are open to that opinion.

00:21:42.968 --> 00:21:45.095
I have no problem with that opinion.”

00:21:45.095 --> 00:21:48.515
I was like, “Nobody's hurt because
of how you feel about the flag.

00:21:48.515 --> 00:21:53.186
We actually know that about you and expect
that from you. But it's more about, you

00:21:53.186 --> 00:21:55.939
know, acknowledging what it may,

00:21:55.939 --> 00:22:00.027
what it may mean to me or
what it may not mean to me.

00:22:00.027 --> 00:22:04.823
Or what it—not even about the flag, just
about the entire demonstration in

00:22:04.823 --> 00:22:09.494
general, what that's about. Acknowledging
that, like, pain or that gripe

00:22:09.494 --> 00:22:12.497
or grievance that I have is,
is really what it comes down to.”

00:22:12.497 --> 00:22:16.501
And I think obviously with all
of the backlash, with him talking,

00:22:16.501 --> 00:22:20.005
you know, to a lot of the guys that
he's been closest with and the guys

00:22:20.005 --> 00:22:23.967
that he's played with, you know, over his long
career—he reached out to a lot

00:22:23.967 --> 00:22:27.012
of us, me included, and, and I think

00:22:27.012 --> 00:22:30.307
guys being able to tell somebody
like Drew Brees the truth, that,

00:22:30.307 --> 00:22:33.435
like, “Your words hurt because of this,”

00:22:33.435 --> 00:22:39.232
like, this was, you know, I think that that
helped him really, one, realize how deep

00:22:39.232 --> 00:22:43.945
it was for his teammates and for other
people that didn't share his experience.

00:22:43.945 --> 00:22:49.451
And I think, you know, he understands that
sometimes, you know, there's opportunity

00:22:49.451 --> 00:22:54.289
for you to really just listen and, and
be a lot more careful, especially as

00:22:54.289 --> 00:22:57.459
polarized as our country is, you
know, when leaders come out and

00:22:57.459 --> 00:23:01.963
speak, you have different factions
trying to attach themselves to,

00:23:01.963 --> 00:23:03.840
like, these figureheads, right?

00:23:03.840 --> 00:23:07.677
Which we saw Trump try to jump
right on Drew's comments.

00:23:07.677 --> 00:23:10.097
And I'm glad that, that Drew
stood up to Trump and said, “No.”

00:23:10.097 --> 00:23:13.100
- Me too. [Laughing] Yeah.
- That was an important step.

00:23:13.100 --> 00:23:16.395
And I think that that shows
that he at least understands

00:23:16.395 --> 00:23:19.481
that, one, there's things
for him to learn, but, two,

00:23:19.481 --> 00:23:21.233
like, this,

00:23:21.233 --> 00:23:23.193
these aren't made-up stories.

00:23:23.193 --> 00:23:25.904
People have been
complaining for centuries

00:23:25.904 --> 00:23:28.490
that there is a real issue
here, and it's time for us

00:23:28.490 --> 00:23:32.786
to really, truly understand the depths of it.

00:23:32.786 --> 00:23:37.749
- The Drew Brees video ended up being
pretty necessary in a lot of ways.

00:23:37.791 --> 00:23:41.503
Cuz it kind of served as a
catalyst for some other things.

00:23:41.503 --> 00:23:46.049
Obviously we saw a lot of NFL stars come
together and make the video to Roger Goodell.

00:23:46.049 --> 00:23:49.344
You know, with Michael Thomas, Saquon
Barkley, Odell, everybody joining

00:23:49.344 --> 00:23:54.975
that video, and Roger Goodell then
responded with a video as well.

00:23:54.975 --> 00:23:58.937
Do you feel like that was a genuine move,

00:23:58.937 --> 00:24:03.358
on the NFL’s part?

00:24:03.358 --> 00:24:08.071
- I think, you know, even if it was,
and I've said this before, until

00:24:08.071 --> 00:24:12.784
they address the Colin Kaepernick
thing, nobody's really going to,

00:24:12.784 --> 00:24:16.496
like, acknowledge the efforts they
put forth. You know, they've, they’ve

00:24:16.496 --> 00:24:17.873
given money in the past.

00:24:17.873 --> 00:24:19.833
They’ve created entire

00:24:19.833 --> 00:24:23.670
“Inspire Change” things. You know, coming out
with statements, you know, don't matter.

00:24:23.670 --> 00:24:27.591
What players really want to see is,
like, how are you going to address

00:24:27.591 --> 00:24:28.592
Colin Kaepernick

00:24:28.592 --> 00:24:32.471
now that you had this awakening of, you
know, how you should have done better,

00:24:32.471 --> 00:24:34.306
and then, two, like, what does that look like?

00:24:34.306 --> 00:24:37.559
Not only with just cutting checks
for charities, but what does it look

00:24:37.559 --> 00:24:39.603
like within your hiring practices?

00:24:39.603 --> 00:24:43.398
There's, like, four Black
coaches and two Black GMs.

00:24:43.398 --> 00:24:46.568
When are you going to start
adding leadership into

00:24:46.568 --> 00:24:51.114
your, you know, front offices?
That's what people want to see.

00:24:51.114 --> 00:24:53.283
 The statements, really, you
know, don't matter.

00:24:53.283 --> 00:24:57.454
And I think the more and more we kind
of get caught up in all of these statements,

00:24:57.454 --> 00:25:02.626
it's, like, the less time we actually
spend as athletes, too, talking about

00:25:02.626 --> 00:25:06.463
the actual, like, issues and what we
need to be drawing attention to.

00:25:06.463 --> 00:25:10.675
So I think it's one
of those things that it’s—

00:25:10.675 --> 00:25:16.181
I'd rather them not make statements
because when they do the whole country

00:25:16.223 --> 00:25:22.145
focuses back on the NFL, what they're
doing, when in reality, we need to be

00:25:22.145 --> 00:25:27.943
pushing our focus to legislation, to this,
to getting people registered to vote

00:25:27.943 --> 00:25:29.152
in this election that's coming up.

00:25:29.152 --> 00:25:32.155
Like, there are so many things
that we need to be focused on.

00:25:32.155 --> 00:25:34.824
And I think every time

00:25:34.824 --> 00:25:39.162
the NFL chimes in on, you
know, what's happening, it just

00:25:39.162 --> 00:25:43.583
leads to more questions and more
scrutiny and more distraction.

00:25:43.583 --> 00:25:46.753
 - Yeah, I mean, I will say, I think it's a
double-edged sword in a lot of ways, right?

00:25:46.753 --> 00:25:52.050
Because I agree and I commend you on
how, whenever these things happen, you're

00:25:52.050 --> 00:25:53.760
like, “OK, what's the actionable item?”

00:25:53.802 --> 00:25:54.636
That's always your thing.

00:25:54.636 --> 00:25:55.595
What can I do to help?

00:25:55.595 --> 00:25:58.723
What can I do to, you know, for the
advancement of Black people? We have

00:25:58.723 --> 00:26:04.396
talked at length, you know, with,
like, things that you can actually do.

00:26:04.396 --> 00:26:05.855
And I think that's great,
and it's important.

00:26:05.855 --> 00:26:10.986
And it's probably what is the most
important. But on the other side, one of

00:26:10.986 --> 00:26:16.032
the main things for me throughout this
time has been that places and people need

00:26:16.032 --> 00:26:18.994
to acknowledge things that they have done.

00:26:18.994 --> 00:26:24.082
Cuz acknowledgement, I think, is a big
part of healing for a lot of people.

00:26:24.082 --> 00:26:29.296
And for me, that is what has been lacking
for the NFL, is the acknowledgement,

00:26:29.296 --> 00:26:32.257
because, and correct me if I'm wrong,

00:26:32.257 --> 00:26:39.347
I think the NFL has played
a role in perpetuating

00:26:39.347 --> 00:26:46.438
either Black people staying stagnant or
in perpetuating some racist ideologies.

00:26:46.438 --> 00:26:49.983
I think it's important for them to
have to say something about that.

00:26:49.983 --> 00:26:53.695
- Yeah. I think, I think if you were looking
at, if you wanted to measure them

00:26:53.695 --> 00:26:58.908
on the diversity in their hiring
practices, outside of the players,

00:26:58.908 --> 00:27:03.913
I would say that there's definitely
a need for more diversity, and that

00:27:03.913 --> 00:27:09.836
could be perpetuating a racist kind
of, you know, organization. Not that

00:27:09.836 --> 00:27:13.256
they, I don't think they would consider
themselves racist, but if you divide

00:27:13.256 --> 00:27:15.800
them up by race, it's telling.

00:27:15.800 --> 00:27:19.929
I think if you look at, um, you
know, obviously Colin Kaepernick

00:27:19.929 --> 00:27:24.976
and what he did and what that in context
now, like, you have police officers,

00:27:24.976 --> 00:27:29.356
people taking knees all over the
world, because it stood for something,

00:27:29.356 --> 00:27:32.859
um, I think that they contributed in,

00:27:32.859 --> 00:27:37.822
um, or, or allowed that message
to be muddied up, and that

00:27:37.822 --> 00:27:39.574
hurt kind of that movement.

00:27:39.574 --> 00:27:44.621
And I think they just, they
just flat-out owe Kaepernick an apology.

00:27:44.621 --> 00:27:46.373
That's the acknowledgement

00:27:46.373 --> 00:27:48.208
I think that needs to happen.

00:27:48.208 --> 00:27:49.209
- [Taylor:] Yeah.

00:27:49.209 --> 00:27:52.962
- Cuz, you know, that's really
what everybody wants to hear.

00:27:52.962 --> 00:27:54.547
The rest of it is cool.

00:27:54.547 --> 00:27:56.341
Everybody's having an awakening.

00:27:56.341 --> 00:28:00.136
I don't think anybody even, like, holds you
to that, but they had an opportunity to

00:28:00.136 --> 00:28:02.347
really be out in front and be proactive.

00:28:02.347 --> 00:28:05.100
And that's the most unfortunate
thing I think about our league

00:28:05.100 --> 00:28:06.810
and leadership in our leagues.

00:28:06.810 --> 00:28:12.315
We haven't really been very proactive
when it comes to things that ail the

00:28:12.315 --> 00:28:15.026
Black community, or really not even
the Black community, just in general.

00:28:15.026 --> 00:28:16.444
We were late on domestic violence.

00:28:16.444 --> 00:28:20.990
We were late with even the
concussion stuff. They were hiding things.

00:28:20.990 --> 00:28:24.911
And so for us, I think until

00:28:24.911 --> 00:28:28.498
you change up or add some
diversity to your leadership,

00:28:28.540 --> 00:28:30.709
you won't have a diversity of perspective.

00:28:30.709 --> 00:28:34.462
You'll always be reacting to, especially
in the Black community, because you

00:28:34.462 --> 00:28:38.091
don't have any Black representation
in leadership and the ability

00:28:38.091 --> 00:28:42.095
to, you know, have a voice at the
table outside of your players.

00:28:42.137 --> 00:28:46.474
And I think that's really the most
telling thing is how do you add

00:28:46.474 --> 00:28:52.605
diversity and Black people to your
leadership to address, you know, the issues that are plaguing

00:28:52.605 --> 00:28:54.816
the communities of 70 percent of your players?

00:28:54.816 --> 00:28:55.567
- Absolutely.

00:28:55.567 --> 00:28:56.443
Absolutely.

00:28:56.443 --> 00:28:58.069
Um, couple more for you.

00:28:58.069 --> 00:29:02.741
Obviously, you know, I think that
right now we're being fed, you know, a

00:29:02.741 --> 00:29:10.290
lot of images and videos that are about
trauma that Black people see and withhold.

00:29:10.290 --> 00:29:14.252
And I think what's getting
lost is that, you know, there

00:29:14.252 --> 00:29:17.213
is so much joy in being Black.

00:29:17.213 --> 00:29:20.967
So I want you to tell me this time, kind of,
what do you love about being Black?

00:29:20.967 --> 00:29:23.678
Cuz I think that that's
equally as important.

00:29:23.678 --> 00:29:31.394
- Yeah. I think, man, Black people are
so resourceful, creative—like, you

00:29:31.394 --> 00:29:37.025
know, for us to have so much, uh,
you know, trauma, to feel oppressed

00:29:37.025 --> 00:29:39.527
and all these things that we have,

00:29:39.527 --> 00:29:43.823
out of those same, you know,
places have come, you know, things

00:29:43.823 --> 00:29:46.868
that have changed the world, come the
visions that have changed the world.

00:29:46.868 --> 00:29:51.164
Hip-hop and all of the dances that
spread around the world start in our

00:29:51.164 --> 00:29:56.002
neighborhoods; our art, our music, like,
all of those things; the joy and love

00:29:56.002 --> 00:29:59.798
that we see within Black families, Black
homes, Black communities; the way that we

00:29:59.798 --> 00:30:03.635
interact with each other that other
people sometimes don't understand is,

00:30:03.718 --> 00:30:06.596
it's something that makes us beautiful.

00:30:06.596 --> 00:30:07.222
And I love it,

00:30:07.222 --> 00:30:10.892
you know, personally. And I think,
you know, for a long time, and even

00:30:10.892 --> 00:30:13.311
still now, when you try to celebrate,

00:30:13.311 --> 00:30:17.315
like, your Blackness and be
proud of your Blackness,

00:30:17.315 --> 00:30:22.487
sometimes people take that as being, you
know, anti-white or anti-everybody else.

00:30:22.487 --> 00:30:24.948
And it's like, no, we
have to understand that

00:30:25.990 --> 00:30:30.453
Black people and our image, how we've
been depicted for, since we stepped

00:30:30.453 --> 00:30:34.791
foot into this country, has always
made us, you know, depicted us

00:30:34.791 --> 00:30:38.044
as less than people. Literally in the
Constitution we’re less than a man.

00:30:38.503 --> 00:30:43.508
And so here we are now in 2020, having to
purposely affirm ourselves, having to

00:30:43.508 --> 00:30:48.805
lift up those things that are beautiful about
us, lift up each other and celebrate

00:30:48.805 --> 00:30:55.353
our culture, our history, our skin, our
hair, our dance, everything about it because

00:30:55.353 --> 00:30:58.773
we know wholeheartedly that the world
will tell us that those things, you

00:30:58.773 --> 00:31:00.984
know, are not great, are not beautiful.

00:31:00.984 --> 00:31:05.238
And I think we went through
really a couple generations that

00:31:05.238 --> 00:31:08.241
tried to do everything, you know, white.

00:31:08.241 --> 00:31:11.035
Tried to change their hair,
change their skin color.

00:31:11.035 --> 00:31:16.541
And I think there's, you know, this
resurgence of this pride in who we are.

00:31:16.541 --> 00:31:19.627
And I think that is the
essence of being Black.

00:31:19.669 --> 00:31:22.547
Is it to be able to maintain
your joy and be able to create

00:31:22.547 --> 00:31:27.343
great things, to be able to thrive and have joy, regardless of

00:31:27.343 --> 00:31:29.387
what the circumstances are around you.

00:31:29.387 --> 00:31:30.388
- Yeah, for sure.

00:31:30.388 --> 00:31:36.728
I think we have a gift to
thrive despite our surroundings.

00:31:36.728 --> 00:31:41.065
And I think that that's super important,
and that shouldn't get lost on anybody.

00:31:41.065 --> 00:31:45.486
I always say, it's like, at the end of the
day, every good quality about myself,

00:31:45.486 --> 00:31:47.989
I feel like I owe to being Black.

00:31:47.989 --> 00:31:56.039
And regardless of the, you know, injustice
that we face or the hate that we may face,

00:31:56.039 --> 00:31:59.709
I would never want to be anything else.

00:31:59.709 --> 00:32:03.504
And I truly mean that.
I don't know what life is without

00:32:03.504 --> 00:32:05.548
Blackness being at my core.

00:32:05.548 --> 00:32:08.760
And I try to, like, implore people
throughout this time to, like, know

00:32:08.760 --> 00:32:11.346
that and remember that because it's so
easy to feel like maybe life could

00:32:11.346 --> 00:32:14.974
have been easier if it was this or
that, but, like, you'd be so different.

00:32:14.974 --> 00:32:17.852
And there's just so many positives.

00:32:17.852 --> 00:32:19.812
And so much strength comes from it.

00:32:19.812 --> 00:32:24.108
And I think, I think that's really
important to say as often as we can

00:32:24.108 --> 00:32:28.279
that, yes, we are these things, but
we're other things too and to not

00:32:28.279 --> 00:32:30.782
be too, um, too caught up in that.

00:32:30.782 --> 00:32:32.492
So those words are great.

00:32:32.492 --> 00:32:35.745
So I'm ending all of these with

00:32:35.745 --> 00:32:38.331
the same question. Just like
I begin with the same one, I'm

00:32:38.331 --> 00:32:39.624
ending with the same as well.

00:32:40.041 --> 00:32:44.754
So words, of course, mean
different things to different people.

00:32:44.921 --> 00:32:47.674
How do you define "America"?

00:32:48.091 --> 00:32:50.969
- I think that there's, it's
changed over the years.

00:32:50.969 --> 00:32:55.640
Um...um.

00:32:55.640 --> 00:33:01.521
I think I see America as a place

00:33:01.521 --> 00:33:06.693
that I'm constantly trying to make home.

00:33:06.693 --> 00:33:12.323
Like, that's, that's how I feel 
about it, is that, you know, this,

00:33:12.323 --> 00:33:14.492
uh. I'm obviously American.

00:33:14.492 --> 00:33:15.910
This is where I live.

00:33:15.910 --> 00:33:20.123
This is the country I
affiliate with. This is where I

00:33:20.123 --> 00:33:23.334
pay taxes. But something about it

00:33:23.334 --> 00:33:29.298
has not quite felt like,
you know, it's made for me.

00:33:29.298 --> 00:33:33.511
And I think a lot of Black
people can say that they have been

00:33:33.511 --> 00:33:38.766
trying to make this place ours, to,
you know, to make sure that we fight

00:33:38.766 --> 00:33:40.476
for the same rights as everybody else

00:33:40.476 --> 00:33:45.565
cuz we built this country. But
that is not necessarily reality.

00:33:45.565 --> 00:33:48.359
And so, it's home by definition,

00:33:48.359 --> 00:33:53.614
but I'm constantly trying to make
it my home, if that makes any sense.

00:33:53.614 --> 00:33:54.157
- Yeah.

00:33:54.157 --> 00:33:57.160
A place I'm always trying
to make home, for sure.

00:33:57.160 --> 00:33:59.328
Thank you, Malcolm.

00:33:59.328 --> 00:34:00.329
- Thank you.

